// SPDX-License-Identifier: CC-BY-SA-4.0
// Expected: a0 at one session and at two.
//
// session_nonce_cross.vp carried out through SIGNVERIF instead of ASSERT. Bob
// signs the concatenation of the challenge he received and a nonce of his own,
// and Alice verifies it. With one Bob the only signature that satisfies her
// check is the one he honestly produced for her, and reproducing it is a
// replay rather than a forgery.
//
// With two Bobs the attacker can feed session 1's challenge to session 2's
// Bob and bring the resulting signature over CONCAT(na, nb#2) back to session
// 1's Alice. sk never leaves Bob, so nothing is forged: Bob#2 is another run
// of the same Bob under the same peer binding, it signed the challenge Alice
// actually sent, and Alice accepts it once. Her acceptance has that run as its
// unique match, so the query holds at both session counts.
//
// The two code paths are why this model exists beside session_nonce_cross.vp.
// ASSERT compares two terms the recipient built, while SIGNVERIF runs a
// rewrite rule with its own matching and filter, and the matching-run rule has
// to reach the second one too.

attacker[active]

principal Bob[
	knows private sk
	generates nb
	pk = PUBKEY(sk)
]

Bob -> Alice: [pk]

principal Alice[
	generates na
]

Alice -> Bob: na

principal Bob[
	sig = SIGN(sk, CONCAT(na, nb))
]

Bob -> Alice: sig, nb

principal Alice[
	_ = SIGNVERIF(pk, CONCAT(na, nb), sig)?
	_ = HASH(sig)
]

queries[
	authentication? Bob -> Alice: sig
]
